    {
      "name": "Radiation Therapy",
      "type": "Method",
      "description": "A cancer treatment that uses high-energy radiation from x-rays, gamma rays, protons, and other sources to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors. It is also used to treat pain caused by cancer. Treatment sessions typically last 30 to 60 minutes, with about 5 sessions per week. Side effects can include skin rash, problems swallowing, dry mouth, thick saliva, changes in taste, and tiredness. Some side effects may occur years later."
    },
    {
      "name": "Systemic Therapy",
      "type": "Method",
      "description": "Treatment with substances that travel in the bloodstream, reaching and affecting cells throughout the body. Types include chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy. Side effects can include tiredness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, hair loss, mouth sores, loss of appetite, and low blood cell counts. Some side effects may occur years later."
    },
    {
      "name": "Targeted Therapy",
      "type": "Method",
      "description": "A type of systemic therapy that targets and attacks specific types of cancer cells. Generally used for thyroid cancers that can't be treated with surgery or RAI therapy, have returned after treatment, or have metastasized and are continuing to grow. The targeted therapies currently used for thyroid cancer are kinase inhibitors, mostly capsules that are swallowed. Side effects can include body aches, rash, high blood pressure, and abnormal bleeding, and some can affect the heart, skin, and digestive system."
    },
    {
      "name": "Chemotherapy",
      "type": "Method",
      "description": "A type of systemic therapy that uses drugs to kill cancer cells. It doesn't work well against most thyroid cancers but may be used for cancer not responding to other treatment or that has spread. Often used in combination with radiation therapy for anaplastic thyroid cancer. Most chemotherapy medicines are put directly into the bloodstream through a vein. Long-term side effects can include other cancers, heart disease, and infertility."
